
  [1mMy recommendation is the second.[0m "Track who logged in" is an identity you learn [3mfrom[0m authenticating, and a parameter
  can only carry an identity the caller already had. But if you have an id at the form layer that I can't see in this
  repo, the first option is right and I'm wrong about your setup.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where does the userId come from? → We want to know who logged in, and it should persist. It should work across [39m
     [38;5;246mthe app, and other forms will need it later.[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m That answer changes the classification. Persisting an identity, available across the app, consumed by forms that don't
  exist yet — that's a shared identity/session layer, which is structure this repo doesn't have. It isn't a parameter
  on [38;5;153mlogin[39m anymore; the parameter would just be one visible edge of it.

  [1mEscalating from bounded to architectural.[0m The ratchet is one-way, so I'm switching to the full path: clarifying
  questions, then 2-3 approaches with trade-offs, then a sectioned design you approve, then a written spec at
  [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-30-login-identity-design.md[39m — and only after you approve that do we get to an
  implementation plan. No code until then.

  Worth naming what you're working with: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a single flat file with a stubbed [38;5;153mlogin[39m that never calls
  [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, and an unrelated [38;5;153msrc/[39m CommonJS module. There's no module system on the page, no build step,
  no router, no state container. So "works across the app" has to be designed rather than plugged in.

  First question, and I think it's the one that most shapes everything else:

  [1mWhat does the rest of the app need the identity for?[0m Two quite different systems come out of this:

  - [1mAttribution only[0m — other forms stamp submissions with who sent them. The identity is a value you read. Nothing
    breaks if it's missing; the form just submits unattributed.
  - [1mAccess state[0m — the app behaves differently logged in vs. out: views gate on it, a logout exists, an expired or
    missing identity has to redirect or re-prompt.

  The first is a small store. The second brings in session lifetime, expiry, and a logout path, and every consumer needs
  a "not logged in" branch. Which one are you actually building toward?

